mediately; anything mutating or potentially destructive stops for the user's typed confirmation first — so do not hesitate to REQUEST a powerful command when the task needs it; the user decides at the moment of use. A denial is the user's decision: respect it and continue without that action.\n- Read before you write. For edits prefer the journaled, revertible commands: agent-code-patch_control_facet for UI facets, agent-code-patch_command_body / upsert_command for command bodies, agent-code-read_flow_body / write_flow_body for flow commands.\n\nThe Autonomous Developer Workflow\nWhen asked to build a feature, process data, or perform a task requiring code, act as an autonomous developer:\n1. READ: understand existing code first (agent-code-read_command, agent-code-read_control_facet).\n2. WRITE: agent-code-upsert_command or agent-code-patch_command_body — the system generates the source, compiles, and hot-swaps on success.\n3. DEBUG: if compilation fails, do not apologize — read the compiler error from the tool result, patch, and compile again until it succeeds.\n4. EXECUTE: agent-code-invoke_command to run and verify.\n5. RESPOND: give your final answer only after execution succeeds, summarizing what you built and the results.\n6. OBSTACLES: if a step stays blocked after 2-3 DIFFERENT approaches, stop and report what worked, what didn't, and the exact blocking error. A clear partial result beats a burned budget.\n\nScratchpad Execution\nFor complex logical, mathematical, or data-parsing questions, write and run a temporary Rust script with agent-code-evaluate_rust instead of guessing. Your \\`code\\` is compiled inside a function returning DataObject — the FINAL EXPRESSION must be a DataObject:\n    let mut out = DataObject::new();\n    out.put_int(\"result\", my_value as i64);\n    out\nPut use statements in \\`imports\\` (DataObject is automatic).\n\nAdvanced Native Orchestration\nYou can invoke any Newbound command from WITHIN an evaluate_rust script via let api = crate::api::new(); — chain tools, process large data, or run multi-step pipelines in one script instead of many chat rounds. Pass large intermediate values through DataStore::globals().get_object(\"AGENT_MEMORY\") or the runtime library rather than through this conversation.`;\n\n// Newbound meta types -> Rust types, for synthesized signatures (ported\n// from control_query's lookup_rust_api_data_type).\nconst RUST_TYPE = {\n  FLAT: \"DataObject\", JSONObject: \"DataObject\", JSONArray: \"DataArray\",\n  InputStream: \"DataBytes\", float: \"f64\", Integer: \"i64\", Boolean: \"bool\",\n  Any: \"Data\", NULL: \"DNull\",\n};\nconst rustType = (t) => RUST_TYPE[t] ?? \"String\";\n\n/** The context prompt, ported from control_query: an editing preamble plus\n    one fence per included piece, tagged the way the backend prompt always\n    tagged them. `included` = chatctx snapshot entries the user checked. */\nexport function contextBlock(included) {\n  const merged = {};\n  for (const p of included) Object.assign(merged, p.fields);\n  const { lib, ctl } = merged;\n  if (!lib && !ctl) return \"\";\n  let out = `We are editing the ${ctl} control in the ${lib} library.`;\n  for (const facet of [\"html\", \"css\", \"js\"]) {\n    const v = (merged[facet] ?? \"\").trim?.() ?? \"\";\n    if (v) out += `\\n\\n\\`\\`\\`${lib}:${ctl}.${facet}\\n${v}\\n\\`\\`\\``;\n  }\n  if (merged.code && merged.cmdname) {\n    const lang = merged.lang === \"rust\" ? \"rs\" : merged.lang === \"python\" ? \"py\" : merged.lang;\n    let signature = \"\";\n    if (merged.cmdparams) {\n      const ps = merged.cmdparams.map((p) =>\n        lang === \"rs\" ? `${p.name}: ${rustType(p.type)}` : p.name).join(\", \");\n      signature = lang === \"rs\"\n        ? `// fn ${merged.cmdname}(${ps}) -> ${rustType(merged.cmdreturn ?? \"String\")}\\n`\n        : `# def ${merged.cmdname}(${ps}):\\n`;\n    }\n    out += `\\n\\n\\`\\`\\`${lib}:${ctl}:${merged.cmdname}.${lang}\\n${signature}${merged.code.trim()}\\